{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788225947273,"sessionID":"ses_fa570caecffeqmzUmkVgycH4Xs","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_390a7cbe626c4da8950fc858","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"\"\"\"REST API service for managing a book collection.\n\nEndpoints:\n    GET    /health         Health check\n    POST   /books          Create a new book\n    GET    /books          List all books (optional ?author= filter)\n    GET    /books/<id>     Get a single book\n    PUT    /books/<id>     Update a book\n    DELETE /books/<id>     Delete a book\n\nData is stored in an embedded SQLite database (path configurable via the\nBOOKS_DB environment variable, defaults to ./books.db).\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport os\nimport sqlite3\nfrom typing import Any\n\nfrom flask import Blueprint, Flask, current_app, g, jsonify, request\n\nDEFAULT_DB_PATH = os.environ.get(\"BOOKS_DB\", \"books.db\")\n\nSCHEMA = \"\"\"\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS books (\n    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\n    title TEXT NOT NULL,\n    author TEXT NOT NULL,\n    year INTEGER,\n    isbn TEXT\n)\n\"\"\"\n\nbp = Blueprint(\"books\", __name__)\n\n\ndef _validate_payload(data: Any) -> tuple[dict[str, Any] | None, str | None]:\n    \"\"\"Validate a book payload.\n\n    Returns (book, None) on success or (None, error_message) on failure.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(data, dict):\n        return None, \"Request body must be a JSON object.\"\n\n    errors: list[str] = []\n\n    title = data.get(\"title\")\n    if not isinstance(title, str) or not title.strip():\n        errors.append(\"'title' is required and must be a non-empty string.\")\n\n    author = data.get(\"author\")\n    if not isinstance(author, str) or not author.strip():\n        errors.append(\"'author' is required and must be a non-empty string.\")\n\n    year = data.get(\"year\")\n    if year is not None and (isinstance(year, bool) or not isinstance(year, int)):\n        errors.append(\"'year' must be an integer.\")\n\n    isbn = data.get(\"isbn\")\n    if isbn is not None and not isinstance(isbn, str):\n        errors.append(\"'isbn' must be a string.\")\n\n    if errors:\n        return None, \" \".join(errors)\n\n    return (\n        {\n            \"title\": title.strip(),\n            \"author\": author.strip(),\n            \"year\": year,\n            \"isbn\": isbn,\n        },\n        None,\n    )\n\n\ndef _row_to_book(row: sqlite3.Row) -> dict[str, Any]:\n    return {\n        \"id\": row[\"id\"],\n        \"title\": row[\"title\"],\n        \"author\": row[\"author\"],\n        \"year\": row[\"year\"],\n        \"isbn\": row[\"isbn\"],\n    }\n\n\ndef get_db() -> sqlite3.Connection:\n    \"\"\"Return the SQLite connection for the current request context.\"\"\"\n    if \"db\" not in g:\n        g.db = sqlite3.connect(current_app.config[\"DATABASE\"])\n        g.db.row_factory = sqlite3.Row\n        g.db.execute(SCHEMA)\n        g.db.commit()\n    return g.db\n\n\ndef close_db(_exc: BaseException | None = None) -> None:\n    db = g.pop(\"db\", None)\n    if db is not None:\n        db.close()\n\n\n@bp.route(\"/health\", methods=[\"GET\"])\ndef health():\n    try:\n        get_db().execute(\"SELECT 1\").fetchone()\n    except sqlite3.Error:\n        return jsonify({\"status\": \"error\", \"database\": \"unavailable\"}), 500\n    return jsonify({\"status\": \"ok\"}), 200\n\n\n@bp.route(\"/books\", methods=[\"POST\"])\ndef create_book():\n    book, error = _validate_payload(request.get_json(silent=True))\n    if error:\n        return jsonify({\"error\": error}), 400\n    db = get_db()\n    cur = db.execute(\n        \"INSERT INTO books (title, author, year, isbn) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)\",\n        (book[\"title\"], book[\"author\"], book[\"year\"], book[\"isbn\"]),\n    )\n    db.commit()\n    row = db.execute(\"SELECT * FROM books WHERE id = ?\", (cur.lastrowid,)).fetchone()\n    return jsonify(_row_to_book(row)), 201\n\n\n@bp.route(\"/books\", methods=[\"GET\"])\ndef list_books():\n    db = get_db()\n    author = request.args.get(\"author\")\n    if author:\n        escaped = (\n            author.strip().lower()\n            .replace(\"\\\\\", \"\\\\\\\\\")\n            .replace(\"%\", \"\\\\%\")\n            .replace(\"_\", \"\\\\_\")\n        )\n        rows = db.execute(\n            \"SELECT * FROM books WHERE lower(author) LIKE ? ESCAPE '\\\\' ORDER BY id\",\n            (f\"%{escaped}%\",),\n        ).fetchall()\n    else:\n        rows = db.execute(\"SELECT * FROM books ORDER BY id\").fetchall()\n    return jsonify([_row_to_book(row) for row in rows]), 200\n\n\n@bp.route(\"/books/<int:book_id>\", methods=[\"GET\"])\ndef get_book(book_id: int):\n    row = get_db().execute(\"SELECT * FROM books WHERE id = ?\", (book_id,)).fetchone()\n    if row is None:\n        return jsonify({\"error\": \"Book not found.\"}), 404\n    return jsonify(_row_to_book(row)), 200\n\n\n@bp.route(\"/books/<int:book_id>\", methods=[\"PUT\"])\ndef update_book(book_id: int):\n    db = get_db()\n    row = db.execute(\"SELECT id FROM books WHERE id = ?\", (book_id,)).fetchone()\n    if row is None:\n        return jsonify({\"error\": \"Book not found.\"}), 404\n    book, error = _validate_payload(request.get_json(silent=True))\n    if error:\n        return jsonify({\"error\": error}), 400\n    db.execute(\n        \"UPDATE books SET title = ?, author = ?, year = ?, isbn = ? WHERE id = ?\",\n        (book[\"title\"], book[\"author\"], book[\"year\"], book[\"isbn\"], book_id),\n    )\n    db.commit()\n    updated = db.execute(\"SELECT * FROM books WHERE id = ?\", (book_id,)).fetchone()\n    return jsonify(_row_to_book(updated)), 200\n\n\n@bp.route(\"/books/<int:book_id>\", methods=[\"DELETE\"])\ndef delete_book(book_id: int):\n    db = get_db()\n    cur = db.execute(\"DELETE FROM books WHERE id = ?\", (book_id,))\n    db.commit()\n    if cur.rowcount == 0:\n        return jsonify({\"error\": \"Book not found.\"}), 404\n    return \"\", 204\n\n\ndef create_app(db_path: str | None = None) -> Flask:\n    \"\"\"Application factory. Pass db_path to use a custom SQLite file.\"\"\"\n    app = Flask(__name__)\n    app.config[\"DATABASE\"] = db_path or DEFAULT_DB_PATH\n    app.register_blueprint(bp)\n    app.teardown_appcontext(close_db)\n\n    @app.errorhandler(404)\n    def not_found(_e):\n        return jsonify({\"error\": \"Not found.\"}), 404\n\n    @app.errorhandler(405)\n    def method_not_allowed(_e):\n        return jsonify({\"error\": \"Method not allowed.\"}), 405\n\n    @app.errorhandler(500)\n    def server_error(_e):\n        return jsonify({\"error\": \"Internal server error.\"}), 500\n\n    return app\n\n\napp = create_app()\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    port = int(os.environ.get(\"PORT\", \"5000\"))\n    app.run(host=\"0.0.0.0\", port=port)\n","filePath":"/workspace/app.py"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/workspace/app.py","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"app.py","time":{"start":1788225947231,"end":1788225947267}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Flask 3.1.3 is installed. Now let me create the app. I'll write a single `app.py` with Flask + SQLite, using parameterized queries, per-request connection management, and proper status codes.\n\nDesign:\n- POST /books → 201 Created with the book, 400 if validation fails\n- GET /books → 200 list, supports ?author= filter (case-insensitive substring or exact? I'll do case-insensitive substring match — actually let me think. \"?author= filter\" — simplest sensible: case-insensitive partial match is friendly, but exact match is also fine. I'll do case-insensitive partial (LIKE) match, and document it in README.)\n- GET /books/{id} → 200 or 404\n- PUT /books/{id} → 200 or 400/404\n- DELETE /books/{id} → 204 or 404\n- GET /health → 200 {\"status\": \"ok\"}\n- year optional integer, isbn optional string\n- Return JSON errors with error messages.\n\nI'll structure it with a `BookService`-ish separation or just keep it simple with functions. For testability, I'll make the app factory pattern: `create_app(db_path)` so tests can use a temp DB. That's idiomatic Flask.\n\nLet me write app.py:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Book collection REST API backed by SQLite.\"\"\"\n\nimport os\nimport sqlite3\n\nfrom flask import Flask, g, jsonify, request\n\nDATABASE = os.environ.get(\"BOOKS_DB\", \"books.db\")\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n```\n\nBetter: app factory.\n\n```python\ndef create_app(db_path: str | None = None) -> Flask:\n    app = Flask(__name__)\n    app.config[\"DATABASE\"] = db_path or os.environ.get(\"BOOKS_DB\", os.path.join(os.path.dirname(__file__), \"books.db\"))\n    ...\n    return app\n```\n\nWith `g` for the connection per request, and teardown to close. Also init_db function that creates the table.\n\nValidation: title and author required (non-empty strings). year must be an integer if provided. ISBN optional string. Return 400 with error messages.\n\nFor PUT: full update — require title/author too (they're required fields), or partial? Task says \"Update a book\". I'll accept partial updates but if title/author provided they must be non-empty... Hmm. Simpler and defensible: PUT requires the same validation as create (title and author required), since PUT is a full replace. But many implementations allow partial. Let me do: PUT validates that title and author are present and non-empty (full replace semantics), year/isbn optional. That's consistent with \"Include input validation (title and author are required)\". I'll document it.\n\nActually, let me be a bit more flexible: allow partial update but validate types. Hmm, but then \"title and author are required\" validation is ambiguous. Standard REST PUT = replace resource. I'll require title and author in PUT. Document clearly in README. That keeps validation consistent and simple.\n\nFields:\n- title: str, required, non-empty after strip\n- author: str, required, non-empty after strip\n- year: optional int (must be int, not bool)\n- isbn: optional str\n\nStatus codes:\n- POST /books: 201 + Location? Not required, but nice. I'll include the book JSON with id. 400 on validation error. 400 also if body isn't JSON / isn't an object.\n- GET /books: 200\n- GET /books/{id}: 200 / 404\n- PUT /books/{id}: 200 / 400 / 404\n- DELETE /books/{id}: 204 / 404\n- GET /health: 200 {\"status\": \"ok\"}\n\n404 for unknown id should return JSON error too ({\"error\": \"Book not found\"}).\n\nMalformed ID (non-integer) → 404 or 400? Flask converter `<int:book_id>` returns 404 for non-int automatically, which is fine.\n\nInvalid JSON body → Flask raises 415 or 400 with HTML. I should register error handlers to return JSON. Use `request.get_json(silent=True)` and check None → 400 \"Request body must be valid JSON\".\n\nDB connection: sqlite3 with `sqlite3.Row` rows.\n\nConcurrency: fine with per-request connections. Use `PRAGMA foreign_keys` not needed (single table). Use `check_same_thread` default fine since each request creates its own connection in the request thread.\n\nTimestamps: created_at/updated_at optional — keep it minimal but maybe include created_at/updated_at? Not required. Keep it simple: id, title, author, year, isbn. I'll keep it minimal to match the spec.\n\nLet me write the code.\n\napp.py:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"A REST API for managing a book collection.\n\nEndpoints:\n    GET    /health         Health check.\n    POST   /books          Create a book.\n    GET    /books          List books (optional ?author= filter).\n    GET    /books/<id>     Retrieve a single book.\n    PUT    /books/<id>     Update a book.\n    DELETE /books/<id>     Delete a book.\n\nData is stored in an embedded SQLite database.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport os\nimport sqlite3\nfrom typing import Any\n\nfrom flask import Flask, g, jsonify, request\n\nDEFAULT_DB_PATH = os.environ.get(\"BOOKS_DB\", \"books.db\")\n\n\ndef get_db() -> sqlite3.Connection:\n    \"\"\"Return the SQLite connection for the current request.\"\"\"\n    if \"db\" not in g:\n        g.db = sqlite3.connect(\n            current_app.config[\"DATABASE\"],\n        )\n        g.db.row_factory = sqlite3.Row\n        g.db.execute(\"PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL\")\n    return g.db\n```\n\nWait — `current_app` needed inside get_db since g is app-context bound. Fine: `from flask import current_app`.\n\nclose_db teardown_appcontext.\n\ninit_db(app) creates table.\n\nValidation helper:\n\n```python\ndef _validate_payload(data: Any) -> tuple[dict | None, str | None]:\n    if not isinstance(data, dict):\n        return None, \"Request body must be a JSON object.\"\n    errors = []\n    title = data.get(\"title\")\n    author = data.get(\"author\")\n    if not isinstance(title, str) or not title.strip():\n        errors.append(\"'title' is required and must be a non-empty string.\")\n    if not isinstance(author, str) or not author.strip():\n        errors.append(\"'author' is required and must be a non-empty string.\")\n    year = data.get(\"year\")\n    if year is not None and (isinstance(year, bool) or not isinstance(year, int)):\n        errors.append(\"'year' must be an integer.\")\n    isbn = data.get(\"isbn\")\n    if isbn is not None and not isinstance(isbn, str):\n        errors.append(\"'isbn' must be a string.\")\n    if errors:\n        return None, \" \".join(errors)\n    book = {\n        \"title\": title.strip(),\n        \"author\": author.strip(),\n        \"year\": year,\n        \"isbn\": isbn,\n    }\n    return book, None\n```\n\nRoutes:\n\n```python\n@app.route(\"/health\", methods=[\"GET\"])\ndef health():\n    return jsonify({\"status\": \"ok\"}), 200\n\n@app.route(\"/books\", methods=[\"POST\"])\ndef create_book():\n    data = request.get_json(silent=True)\n    book, error = _validate_payload(data)\n    if error:\n        return jsonify({\"error\": error}), 400\n    db = get_db()\n    cur = db.execute(\n        \"INSERT INTO books (title, author, year, isbn) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)\",\n        (book[\"title\"], book[\"author\"], book[\"year\"], book[\"isbn\"]),\n    )\n    db.commit()\n    row = db.execute(\"SELECT * FROM books WHERE id = ?\", (cur.lastrowid,)).fetchone()\n    return jsonify(_row_to_book(row)), 201\n```\n\nList:\n\n```python\n@app.route(\"/books\", methods=[\"GET\"])\ndef list_books():\n    author = request.args.get(\"author\")\n    db = get_db()\n    if author:\n        rows = db.execute(\n            \"SELECT * FROM books WHERE lower(author) LIKE ? ORDER BY id\",\n            (f\"%{author.strip().lower()}%\",),\n        ).fetchall()\n    else:\n        rows = db.execute(\"SELECT * FROM books ORDER BY id\").fetchall()\n    return jsonify([_row_to_book(r) for r in rows]), 200\n```\n\nGet one:\n\n```python\n@app.route(\"/books/<int:book_id>\", methods=[\"GET\"])\ndef get_book(book_id):\n    row = get_db().execute(\"SELECT * FROM books WHERE id = ?\", (book_id,)).fetchone()\n    if row is None:\n        return jsonify({\"error\": \"Book not found.\"}), 404\n    return jsonify(_row_to_book(row)), 200\n```\n\nUpdate (PUT, full replace):\n\n```python\n@app.route(\"/books/<int:book_id>\", methods=[\"PUT\"])\ndef update_book(book_id):\n    db = get_db()\n    row = db.execute(\"SELECT id FROM books WHERE id = ?\", (book_id,)).fetchone()\n    if row is None:\n        return jsonify({\"error\": \"Book not found.\"}), 404\n    data = request.get_json(silent=True)\n    book, error = _validate_payload(data)\n    if error:\n        return jsonify({\"error\": error}), 400\n    db.execute(\n        \"UPDATE books SET title = ?, author = ?, year = ?, isbn = ? WHERE id = ?\",\n        (book[\"title\"], book[\"author\"], book[\"year\"], book[\"isbn\"], book_id),\n    )\n    db.commit()\n    updated = db.execute(\"SELECT * FROM books WHERE id = ?\", (book_id,)).fetchone()\n    return jsonify(_row_to_book(updated)), 200\n```\n\nDelete:\n\n```python\n@app.route(\"/books/<int:book_id>\", methods=[\"DELETE\"])\ndef delete_book(book_id):\n    db = get_db()\n    cur = db.execute(\"DELETE FROM books WHERE id = ?\", (book_id,))\n    db.commit()\n    if cur.rowcount == 0:\n        return jsonify({\"error\": \"Book not found.\"}), 404\n    return \"\", 204\n```\n\nHealth endpoint could also verify DB connectivity — a nice touch: run `SELECT 1`. I'll do that and return 200 if OK, 500 if not. Keep it simple but robust.\n\nApp factory + module-level app for `flask run` / gunicorn:\n\n```python\ndef create_app(db_path: str | None = None) -> Flask:\n    app = Flask(__name__)\n    app.config[\"DATABASE\"] = db_path or DEFAULT_DB_PATH\n    init_db(app.config[\"DATABASE\"])\n    app.teardown_appcontext(close_db)\n    register routes...\n```\n\nRegistering routes inside create_app via a nested function or by using a Blueprint. Blueprint is cleaner: define `bp = Blueprint(\"books\", __name__)` with all routes, then `app.register_blueprint(bp)` in factory. And `app = create_app()` at module bottom for direct run with `python app.py` and for `flask --app app run`.\n\nCareful: health endpoint uses db for check — wrap in try/except.\n\n_row_to_book:\n\n```python\ndef _row_to_book(row: sqlite3.Row) -> dict:\n    return {\n        \"id\": row[\"id\"],\n        \"title\": row[\"title\"],\n        \"author\": row[\"author\"],\n        \"year\": row[\"year\"],\n        \"isbn\": row[\"isbn\"],\n    }\n```\n\ninit_db:\n\n```python\ndef init_db(db_path: str) -> None:\n    conn = sqlite3.connect(db_path)\n    try:\n        conn.execute(\n            \"\"\"\n            CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS books (\n                id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\n                title TEXT NOT NULL,\n                author TEXT NOT NULL,\n                year INTEGER,\n                isbn TEXT\n            )\n            \"\"\"\n        )\n        conn.commit()\n    finally:\n        conn.close()\n```\n\nget_db with current_app:\n\n```python\ndef get_db() -> sqlite3.Connection:\n    if \"db\" not in g:\n        g.db = sqlite3.connect(current_app.config[\"DATABASE\"])\n        g.db.row_factory = sqlite3.Row\n    return g.db\n\ndef close_db(_exc: BaseException | None = None) -> None:\n    db = g.pop(\"db\", None)\n    if db is not None:\n        db.close()\n```\n\nTests (tests.py or test_app.py with pytest):\n- test_health\n- test_create_and_get_book\n- test_create_missing_title_returns_400\n- test_list_books_with_author_filter\n- test_update_book\n- test_delete_book / get deleted → 404\n- test_get_unknown_book_returns_404\n\nUse Flask test_client with temp db via create_app(tmp_path). Use pytest fixture. Use `app.config[\"TESTING\"] = True`.\n\nFor the module-level `app = create_app()`, importing app.py creates books.db in cwd — acceptable, but in tests I'll use factory with tmp_path so no pollution. Actually creating books.db on import is slightly annoying; I can guard: create default DB only when run directly (`if __name__ == \"__main__\"`) plus `flask run` needs it too... With `flask --app app run`, create_app() is called at import time if I do module-level app = create_app(). Hmm. Alternative: lazy init — `init_db` in get_db via `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` on first connection per request? Simpler: in get_db, if \"db\" not in g: connect and run CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS. That makes everything lazy and robust. I'll do that — idempotent, cheap.\n\nSo:\n\n```python\nSCHEMA = \"\"\"...\"\"\"\n\ndef get_db():\n    if \"db\" not in g:\n        g.db = sqlite3.connect(current_app.config[\"DATABASE\"])\n        g.db.row_factory = sqlite3.Row\n        g.db.executescript? or just execute CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS\n        g.db.commit()? not needed for DDL in sqlite (implicit)\n    return g.db\n```\n\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS executes fine without commit (DDL autocommits in sqlite3 default isolation? Actually in Python sqlite3, DDL statements are executed in transaction too before 3.6 autocommit changes; with default isolation_level=\"\" DML opens transaction. CREATE TABLE ... in a transaction that stays open could hold lock. Safer: conn.execute(\"CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS ...\"); conn.commit(). Fine.\n\nActually let me use `isolation_level=None` (autocommit) and explicit BEGIN? Overkill. Keep default, run CREATE TABLE + commit once per new connection.\n\nAlso add requirements.txt with flask and pytest (pytest for dev). Add `.gitignore`? Not required but harmless. Keep deliverables focused: app.py, tests, README.md, requirements.txt.\n\nFor running: `python app.py` runs app.run(host/port from env, port 5000).\n\nAlso README: setup (venv, pip install -r requirements.txt), run instructions, API docs with curl examples, testing instructions.\n\nEdge: DELETE returns 204 with empty body — `return \"\", 204`.\n\nLet me also make sure JSON error handlers for 404/405 return JSON: Flask default returns HTML for 404 on unknown route. I'll add error handlers:\n\n```python\n@app.errorhandler(404)\ndef not_found(e): return jsonify({\"error\": \"Not found.\"}), 404\n@app.errorhandler(405)\ndef method_not_allowed(e): return jsonify({\"error\": \"Method not allowed.\"}), 405\n@app.errorhandler(500)\ndef server_error(e): return jsonify({\"error\": \"Internal server error.\"}), 500\n```\n\nGood. Now write files.\n\nrequirements.txt:\n```\nflask>=3.0\npytest>=8.0\n```\npytest is for tests; fine to include (or separate). Keep single file with comment. Comments not allowed? \"DO NOT ADD ANY COMMENTS unless asked\" — that's for code files. requirements.txt comments... I'll avoid comments there too. Two sections fine without comments.\n\nNow the year validation: bool check — `isinstance(True, int)` is True, so exclude bools explicitly.\n\nIn JSON, year might come as 1997 (int) fine. If float like 1997.0? Reject with message \"must be an integer\". Fine.\n\nAlso what about extra fields? Ignore them. Fine.\n\nISBN: could be int in payload; reject unless string? \"isbn\" optional; if provided must be string. Hmm, some might send numbers. Strictness is fine and documented.\n\nNow write the files.\n\ntests: file name `test_app.py`.\n\n```python\nimport pytest\nfrom app import create_app\n\n\n@pytest.fixture()\ndef client(tmp_path):\n    app = create_app(db_path=str(tmp_path / \"test.db\"))\n    app.config[\"TESTING\"] = True\n    with app.test_client() as client:\n        yield client\n\n\ndef test_health(client):\n    resp = client.get(\"/health\")\n    assert resp.status_code == 200\n    assert resp.get_json() == {\"status\": \"ok\"}\n\n\ndef test_create_and_get_book(client):\n    resp = client.post(\"/books\", json={\"title\": \"Dune\", \"author\": \"Frank Herbert\", \"year\": 1965, \"isbn\": \"978-0441172719\"})\n    assert resp.status_code == 201\n    book = resp.get_json()\n    assert book[\"title\"] == \"Dune\"\n    assert book[\"id\"]\n    resp = client.get(f\"/books/{book['id']}\")\n    assert resp.status_code == 200\n    assert resp.get_json() == book\n\n\ndef test_create_book_requires_title_and_author(client):\n    resp = client.post(\"/books\", json={\"year\": 2000})\n    assert resp.status_code == 400\n    assert \"title\" in resp.get_json()[\"error\"]\n    resp = client.post(\"/books\", json={\"title\": \"X\", \"author\": \"\"})\n    assert resp.status_code == 400\n\n\ndef test_list_books_filter_by_author(client):\n    client.post(\"/books\", json={\"title\": \"Dune\", \"author\": \"Frank Herbert\"})\n    client.post(\"/books\", json={\"title\": \"1984\", \"author\": \"George Orwell\"})\n    client.post(\"/books\", json={\"title\": \"Animal Farm\", \"author\": \"George Orwell\"})\n    resp = client.get(\"/books\", query_string={\"author\": \"orwell\"})\n    assert resp.status_code == 200\n    books = resp.get_json()\n    assert len(books) == 2\n    assert {b[\"title\"] for b in books} == {\"1984\", \"Animal Farm\"}\n\n\ndef test_update_book(client):\n    created = client.post(\"/books\", json={\"title\": \"Old\", \"author\": \"A\"}).get_json()\n    resp = client.put(f\"/books/{created['id']}\", json={\"title\": \"New\", \"author\": \"B\", \"year\": 2001})\n    assert resp.status_code == 200\n    assert resp.get_json()[\"title\"] == \"New\"\n\n\ndef test_delete_book(client):\n    created = client.post(\"/books\", json={\"title\": \"T\", \"author\": \"A\"}).get_json()\n    resp = client.delete(f\"/books/{created['id']}\")\n    assert resp.status_code == 204\n    assert client.get(f\"/books/{created['id']}\").status_code == 404\n\n\ndef test_get_unknown_book_returns_404(client):\n    assert client.get(\"/books/9999\").status_code == 404\n    assert client.get(\"/books/9999\").get_json()[\"error\"]\n```\n\n7 tests, more than 3. \n\nOne nuance: in `test_create_and_get_book`, GET returns book dict; equality with created — same fields, yes since _row_to_book used for both.\n\nUpdate with missing title → 400 test could be added into update test. Also PUT on unknown id → 404. Add asserts.\n\nAlso POST with no JSON body → 400. Add test: client.post(\"/books\") without json → get_json(silent=True) returns None → not dict → 400. \n\nNote: `client.post(\"/books\", json=None)`? Just `client.post(\"/books\", data=\"\")`. Let me add to the validation test: `resp = client.post(\"/books\", data=\"not json\", content_type=\"application/json\")` → silent=True → None → 400. Good.\n\nNow README.md: overview, requirements, setup, run, endpoints table, curl examples, testing.\n\nAlso add a `books.db` to .gitignore? Creating .gitignore is reasonable; not requested. Skip? \"Write all code files to the current directory\" — deliverables: source code, README, tests. I'll add requirements.txt and a minimal .gitignore for books.db/__pycache__ — harmless and useful. I'll include it.\n\nNow write app.py carefully.\n\nOne more consideration on `DEFAULT_DB_PATH = os.environ.get(\"BOOKS_DB\", \"books.db\")` — relative to cwd. Fine for README (mention BOOKS_DB env var).\n\nHealth check with DB verify:\n\n```python\n@bp.route(\"/health\", methods=[\"GET\"])\ndef health():\n    try:\n        get_db().execute(\"SELECT 1\").fetchone()\n    except sqlite3.Error:\n        return jsonify({\"status\": \"error\", \"database\": \"unavailable\"}), 500\n    return jsonify({\"status\": \"ok\"}), 200\n```\n\nNote: errorhandlers must be on app, not blueprint (blueprint handlers only catch blueprint errors — actually blueprint error handlers do work for exceptions raised in blueprint routes). Flask supports app.errorhandler after create_app; I'll register in create_app.\n\nStructure of app.py:\n\n- imports\n- SCHEMA constant\n- _validate_payload\n- _row_to_book\n- get_db, close_db\n- bp = Blueprint(\"books\", __name__) with routes\n- create_app(db_path=None) -> Flask: config, register blueprint, teardown, error handlers\n- app = create_app() at module level? If tests import create_app only, module-level app = create_app() still runs on import and creates ./books.db in cwd when tests run. Hmm — that creates a stray file during pytest runs in /workspace. Avoid: only create when run as script, and instruct `flask --app app run`? For `flask --app app run`, Flask looks for `app` or `application` in module. If no module-level app, `flask --app app:app` fails... Flask 3 auto-detect: `flask --app app run` → imports `app.py`, looks for `app`/`application`. None → error.\n\nOptions:\n1. Lazy creation: module-level `app = None` + `__getattr__` (module-level PEP 562). Works: Flask does getattr(app_module, \"app\") → triggers __getattr__ → create_app(). Cute but obscure.\n2. Guard env: only create app at import if not under pytest: hacky.\n3. Just create module-level app = create_app() and accept books.db creation in cwd (empty schema). Then .gitignore it. Simple, standard pattern.\n4. create_app lazily initializes table on first connection anyway (get_db does CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS). But create_app() itself would just make config + connect nothing — sqlite file only created on first connect! Right: sqlite3.connect creates the file, but if create_app never connects (no request), no file is created. In my design get_db connects per request, so `create_app()` at import does NOT create books.db. Only running the server / making requests creates it. \n\nSo module-level `app = create_app()` is safe-ish: file only appears after first request. And `if __name__ == \"__main__\": app.run(...)` for `python app.py`.\n\nThat's clean. get_db does CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS + commit on each new connection — slightly wasteful but fine, guarantees schema.\n\nNow finalize code. No comments per instructions.\n\nOne thing: `from flask import Blueprint, current_app, Flask, g, jsonify, request`.\n\nLet me also double check DELETE 204 with `return \"\", 204` — jsonify not needed. Content-Type: text/html on empty body; fine.\n\nGET /books author filter: use LIKE with escaped wildcards? If user passes % or _ it acts as wildcard. Minor; keep simple, document \"case-insensitive substring match\". I could escape: `LIKE ? ESCAPE '\\'` with escaped input. Let me do it properly:\n\n```python\nescaped = author.strip().lower().replace(\"\\\\\", \"\\\\\\\\\").replace(\"%\", \"\\\\%\").replace(\"_\", \"\\\\_\")\nrows = db.execute(\n    \"SELECT * FROM books WHERE lower(author) LIKE ? ESCAPE '\\\\' ORDER BY id\",\n    (f\"%{escaped}%\",),\n).fetchall()\n```\n\nHmm wait, if I lower() in Python and also lower(author) in SQL, that's case-insensitive matching for ASCII. SQLite lower() only handles ASCII; Python side too for input. Good enough; document.\n\nAlso store author as given; filter uses lower(). OK.\n\nRequirements pin: flask>=3.0, pytest>=8.0. Write files now.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05a928652001Tm2J1yAiLFdWOX","sessionID":"ses_fa570caecffeqmzUmkVgycH4Xs","messageID":"msg_05a90209f001IyBls1wAIEcLdZ"}}
